## Third-party components

The self-hosted database image (`timescale/timescaledb-ha:pg17`, pinned in
`docker-compose.prod.yml`, see `organizational/agent-deployment-runbook.md:159-183`)
bundles TimescaleDB. Most of TimescaleDB is Apache License 2.0; the
compression, continuous-aggregate and retention-policy features this project
uses (`packages/db/migrations/0004_rollups_and_retention.sql`) are licensed
under the **Timescale License (TSL)**, source read directly at
<https://github.com/timescale/timescaledb/blob/main/tsl/LICENSE-TIMESCALE>
("Posted Date: September 24, 2020" — the current text at that URL as of
2026-09-13).

**Conclusion, reasoned from the text actually read:** TSL § 2.2 prohibits
offering TSL Licensed Software "to provide time-sharing services or
database-as-a-service services... in which the TSL Licensed Software is
offered or made available to third parties to provide time-series database
functions or operations, other than as part of Your Value Added Products or
Services." § 2.1(b) and the § 3.10 definition permit exactly that
Value-Added-Products carve-out where (i) the product is not primarily a
database product, (ii) it adds substantial value of a different nature, and
(iii) its users are contractually or technically prohibited from issuing
schema-defining commands (the Timescale Data Definition Interfaces) against
the database directly. metrion exposes only an HTTP metrics ingest/query API
(`applications/ingest`, `applications/viewer`) — no SQL surface, no way for
a caller to run DDL or connect to Postgres directly — so no user of the
product can touch the Timescale Data Definition Interfaces at all, which
satisfies (iii) more strongly than the licence requires (technical
impossibility, not just contractual prohibition). On this reading,
self-hosting TimescaleDB behind metrion's own HTTP API is permitted "Value
Added Products or Services" use under TSL § 2.1(b)/§ 3.10, not the
prohibited "database-as-a-service" use under § 2.2. This is a licence-text
reading, not a court's; if the repository is made public on this
conclusion, it is worth a second read by counsel before that publication,
not because the text is ambiguous but because the stakes of being wrong (an
automatically-terminating licence, TSL § 4) are real.
